'''torcular''' (tôr′kū-lär), ''n.'' [< L. ''torcular'', a press used in making wine, < ''torquere'', twist: see ''tort''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. A surgical instrument, the tourniquet.—2. In ''anat.'', the confluence of the venous sinuses in the brain: more fully called ''torcular Herophili.''—{{smaller|'''Torcular Herophili''', in ''anat.'', the wine-press of Herophilus, the place in the meninges of the brain, at the internal occipital protuberance, where the sinus of the falx cerebri joins the lateral sinus of the tentorium cerebelli, and other sinuses meet. This confluence of venous currents was supposed to exert some pressure upon the circulation (whence the name). See ''straight sinus'', under ''sinus''.}}

'''Tordylium''' (tôr-dil′i-um), ''n.'' [NL. (Morison, 1672), < L. ''tordylion'', ''tordylon'', < Gr. τορδύλιον, τόρδυλον, an umbelliferous plant, hartwort.] A genus of umbelliferous plants, of the tribe ''Peucedaneæ''. {{smaller|It is characterized by conspicuous calyx-teeth, marginal petals frequently enlarged and two-lobed, a hirsute ovary, and a fruit with thick and often rugose margin, inconspicuous ridges, and oil-tubes solitary in their channels, or in a few species numerous. There are about 12 species, natives of Europe, northern Africa, and temperate and central parts of Asia. They are hairy annuals, usually bearing pinnate leaves with broad leaflets, or sometimes somewhat cordate undivided leaves. The flowers are white or purplish, and form compound umbels. The species are known as ''hartwort'' (which see).}}

'''tore'''<sup>1</sup> (tōr). Preterit of ''tear''<sup>1</sup>.

'''tore'''<sup>2</sup> (tōr), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''tor'', ''torre''; prob. a particular use of ''tor''<sup>1</sup>, a hill, prominence (W. ''tor'', a knob, boss, etc.): see ''tor''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. A projecting knob or ball used as an ornament on furniture, as cradles and chairs.

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|Hyre ryche robys sche all ''to-rytte'',
And was ravysed out of hyr wytte.
{{right|''MS. Ashmole'' 61, XV. Cent. (''Halliwell'', under ''ritte.'')}}}}

'''torive†''', ''v. t.'' [ME. ''toriven''; < ''to-''<sup>2</sup> + ''rive''<sup>1</sup>.] To rive in pieces; rend.

{{smaller block|The king share thrugh his shild with the sharpe ende,
And the rod all ''to roofe'' right to his honde.
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1234.}}}}

'''torment''' (tôr′ment), ''n.'' [< ME. ''torment, tourment, turment'', < OF. ''torment, tourment, turment'', F. ''tourment'' = Pr. ''torment, turment'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''tormento'', torment (cf. Sp. Pg. ''tormenta'', a tempest), < L. ''tormentum'', an engine for hurling stones, a missile so hurled, also an instrument of torture, a rack, hence torture, anguish, torment, also a mangle, clothes-press, also a cord, rope, < ''torquere'', twist, hurl, throw, rack, torture, torment: see ''tort''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''torture.''] '''1†.''' An engine of war for casting stones, darts, or other missiles; a tormentum.

{{smaller block|Vitruuius . . . sayth, All ''turmentes'' of warre, whiche we cal ordinance, were first inuented by kinges or gouernours of hostes. {{right|''Sir T. Elyot'', The Governour, i. 8.}}}}

'''2.''' An instrument of torture, as the rack, the thumbscrew, or the wheel; also, the application of such an instrument, or the torture caused by it.

{{smaller block|Zaynte Agase, thet mid greate blisse . . . yede to ''torment'' alsuo ase hi yede to feste other to a bredale.
{{right|''Ayenbite of Inwyt'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 166.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
''mentare'', torment, twist, < L. ''tormentum'', torment: see ''torment'', ''n.''] 1. To put to torment, as with the rack or the wheel; torture.

{{smaller block|He shall be ''tormented'' with fire and brimstone in the presence of the holy angels. {{right|Rev. xiv. 10.}}}}

2. To bring suffering or misery upon; pain; plague; distress; afflict.

{{smaller block|Thow dosse bot tynnez thi tyme, and ''turmenttez'' thi pople. {{right|''Morte Arthure'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1954.}}}}

{{smaller block|Raw it is no better then poyson, and being rosted, except it be tender and the heat abated, . . . it will prickle and ''torment'' the throat extreamely. {{right|''Capt. John Smith'', Works, I. 123.}}}}

{{smaller block|A provoking gipsy! to run away, and ''torment'' her poor father, that doats on her! {{right|''Colman'', Jealous Wife, ii.}}}}

3. To twist; distort.

{{smaller block|The fix'd and rooted earth,
''Tormented'' into billows, heaves and swells. {{right|''Cowper'', Task, ii. 101.}}}}

{{smaller block|The monument of Margaret [of Bourbon] herself is . . . in white marble, ''tormented'' into a multitude of exquisite patterns. {{right|''H. James, Jr.'', Little Tour, p. 246.}}}}

4. To throw into agitation; disturb greatly. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Then, soaring on main wing,
''Tormented'' all the air. {{right|''Milton'', P. L., vi. 244.}}}}

{{smaller|='''Syn.''' 1. To agonize, rack, excruciate.—2. ''Plague, Worry'', etc. (See ''tease''.) ''Trouble, Distress'', etc. See ''afflict''.}}

'''tormenta''', ''n.'' Plural of ''tormentum''.

'''tormented''' (tôr-men′ted), ''p. a.'' Tortured; agonized; distorted: occasionally used in the United States as a euphemism for ''damned'': as, not a ''tormented'' cent. ''Lowell'', Int. to Biglow Papers.

'''tormenter''' (tôr-men′tėr), ''n.'' [< ''torment'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.]
